     html.P("DATA SOURCE The Stormlight Archive is a bestselling epic fantasy series by Brandon Sanderson. For this project, we analyzed the text of the first two Stormlight novels, The Way of Kings and Words of Radiance. These texts are under copyright. To obtain the texts, we purchased digital copies from ebooks.com. This analysis of copyrighted material constitutes fair usage, and we have been careful to not expose the copyrighted text itself to the public. METHODOLOGY TEXT PARSING The foundational step for each of these analyses was to break the text into several CSV files. Once the digital watermarking was cleared from the text files using regex, we created CSVs breaking the books into chapters, paragraphs, and sentences. STYLOMETRIC ANALYSIS WITH PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS (PCA) To create the stylometric analysis, we used the chapter-level CSV file. PCA analysis takes the n most common terms (we chose 1,000) and turns each of those terms into an axis in n-dimensional space. Each usage of each term pushes a given chapter along that term’s axis. Once each chapter has been placed, the network is projected down to 2-dimensional space. PC1 is the axis along which documents differ the most, PC2 is the next-most-different axis orthogonal to PC1. This creates a scatterplot where the stylometric distinctions between chapters are clearly displayed. KEY DECISIONS Character names were stripped from the vocabulary list. The close third person prose of these novels means that every Kaladin PoV Chapter contains many, many iterations of the word “Kaladin.” Stripping these names prevents this from influencing the PCA placement. Each book's mean feature vector was subtracted from its own chapters. This reduces noise and prevents the stylistic differences between books from overshadowing the stylistic differences between characters. CHARACTER CO-OCCURRENCE GRAPH This graph measures the co-occurrence of characters by chapter. We started with a list of the books’ characters. These are our nodes. Each time that two items from the list co-exist in a chapter, an edge is created between them, marked by chapter so that the user can select any section of the texts. KEY DECISIONS The singular key decision for this plot was to go with co-occurrence rather than dialogue interactions. Trying to accurately pull the dialogue interactions from the text proved too difficult for the scale of this project, and would have required a lot of manual data entry. SENTIMENT ANALYSIS To perform the Sentiment analysis, we used VADER (Valence Aware Dictionary and sEntiment Reasoner) to calculate a sentiment score paragraph-by-paragraph, and then averaged the paragraph scores to calculate a sentiment between 1 and -1 for each chapter. These are then laid out chronologically. We decided to separate and stack the two books to allow the user to compare the two books to each other. KEY DECISIONS We stripped the epigraphs from the chapters, because they were often strong outliers and not necessarily representative of the chapters’ contents. We also stripped dialogue tags and some other common neutral words from the text, to avoid drowning out the words with higher sentiment scores. TOPICS OVER TIME To create this graph we used BERTopic, which takes the text and finds word patterns and co-occurances that would be invisible to the human eye. BERTopic is a transformer, and captures the semantic usage of the words, rather than a simple bag-of-words approach. These patterns resulted in a list of topics, which were algorithmically culled to reduce noise, and then hand-selected to create a list of meaningful topics, which were then manually labeled. KEY DECISIONS We cut character and place names from the text, to prevent names dominating topics.")
